# tools/al/ales_stereo_halve_neg_witness.rish -- stereo_halve_neg: the inverted half-wave rectifier carried into stereo with ONE SHARED THRESHOLD AT ZERO, both channels in lockstep, COMPLETING the rectifier family in
# stereo (ALES140).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus - the creative suite) - waymark ALES - rung ALES140.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-041250_fill-ales140-lotus-stereo-inverted-half-wave.md
# Stands on: lotus/halve_neg.rye (ALES86 -- the mono inverted half-wave rectifier run once per channel, min(x, 0) with no rail edge), lotus/stereo_halve.rye (ALES139 -- the positive half-wave sibling this rung mirrors,
# the two summing to the dry master), lotus/pan.rye (ALES10 -- the StereoClip, two mono Clips left/right, and its balance invariant), lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2 -- the Clip, its buf/len/samples, sample_min/max). A Lotus
# master is a StereoClip whose defining invariant is that left and right hold the same length. This rung carries ALES86's inverted half-wave rectifier into stereo -- keeping the negative half of the wave whole and
# folding the positive half away to silence in both channels so an inverted half-wave rectification lands on both speakers together, the flipped-diode voice. With it the stereo rectifier family (full-wave ALES138,
# positive half-wave ALES139, inverted half-wave here) stands whole: the two half-waves partition the wave, max(x, 0) + min(x, 0) = x.
#
# lotus/stereo_halve_neg.rye validates the span once against the shared length before either channel is mutated (BadRange on a span outside the samples), then inverted-halves both channels through ALES86's halve_neg;
# HalveNegError reused whole (min(x, 0) has no illegal parameter and no rail edge, so no saturate is owed). The crux is four laws. THE STEREO INVERTED-HALVE LAW: each channel equals ALES86's mono halve_neg over the
# same span byte for byte. THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW: left.len == right.len after, both holding their starting length. THE FAMILY-WHOLE / IMAGE-PARTITION LAW: the two stereo half-waves partition the dry master, an
# out-of-phase master returning partitioned and non-positive (left - right = 0 and left + right = −|left|), the ratio held on the kept samples, an identical-channel master staying identical, every output non-positive
# on both, the map idempotent. THE ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: a refusal leaves both channels untouched and balanced, count 0 the identity.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/al/ales_stereo_halve_neg_witness.rish

say "ales-stereo-halve-neg: Language EN — stereo_halve_neg inverted-half-wave-rectifies a span in both channels of a stereo master, keeping the negative half of the wave whole and folding the positive half away to silence, y = min(x, 0), the same threshold at zero on each, so one inverted half-wave rectification lands on both speakers together. A Lotus master is two Clips heard together, one per speaker, whose defining invariant is that left and right hold the same length so the two channels stay aligned in time. This rung completes the stereo rectifier family on ALES86's inverted half-wave rectifier, running the proven mono halve_neg on each channel, validated once against the shared length before either channel is mutated so a refusal never rectifies one channel and leaves the other signed."

say "ales-stereo-halve-neg: Style Radiant — the positive half-wave just carried into stereo keeps the positive half of the wave and silences the negative, and this rung is its mirror: keep the negative half and silence the positive, a single diode flipped the other way, y = min of x and zero. Together the two half-waves partition the wave, max of x and zero plus min of x and zero equals x, the plainest decomposition of a signal into its positive and negative parts, and that is the round's finishing law read in stereo: the family whole. Carried into stereo the two half-waves reconstruct the dry master, stereo_halve on one copy and stereo_halve_neg on another summing sample for sample on each channel back to the original. On an out-of-phase master, right the negation of left, the inverted half-wave returns partitioned like its sibling but on the non-positive side: at each index exactly one channel survives, because a sample and its negation cannot both be negative, so the left keeps its negatives and silences where it was positive while the right keeps its own negatives there. Two facts hold at every index: left times right equals zero, the channels disjoint and never both sounding at once, and left plus right equals the negated magnitude of left, since min of x and zero plus min of minus x and zero equals minus the magnitude of x. Every output on both channels is non-positive, the mirror of the positive half-wave's non-negative one-sided signal. Yet a one to two master keeps its ratio on the kept samples, both going to silence where the left is positive, and an identical-channel master stays identical. The rule is stated positively: min of x and zero is exact for every i16 with no rail edge at all, a non-positive sample passing unchanged and sample_min passing through untouched while a positive becomes zero, so the inverted half-wave needs no saturate, and it names exactly one fault — refuse BadRange before either channel is mutated, else inverted-halve both channels over the span via ALES86's halve_neg. It reuses ALES86's HalveNegError by name and ALES86's halve_neg for the arithmetic, inventing no new fault and no new primitive. The crux stands out as four laws: the stereo inverted-halve law (each channel equals ALES86's mono halve_neg over the same span byte for byte), the balance / length law (left.len == right.len after the edit and both hold their starting length, a halve_neg writing values only), the family-whole / image-partition law (the two stereo half-waves partition the dry master, an out-of-phase master returning partitioned and non-positive with left times right zero and left plus right the negated magnitude, the ratio held on the kept samples, an identical master staying identical, every output non-positive on both, the map idempotent), and the atomicity / degenerate law (any refusal leaves both channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced, count zero the identity on both)."

say "ales-stereo-halve-neg: Lens TAME — two bounded i16 Clips (left, right) inverted-half-wave-rectified in lockstep by one shared threshold at zero; the balance invariant is asserted on entry and exit, the span is bound-checked once before any write, and because that check depends only on the shared length and holds for both channels each mono halve_neg is pre-validated to succeed so no refusal can land between the two channel edits; halve_neg walks the span once per channel, writing min of x and zero, the map memoryless (every output depends only on its own input) and each output non-positive and at least its input, with no rail edge (sample_min passing through) so no saturate is owed, never touching a byte past len so the length holds exactly; it reuses ALES86's halve_neg per channel over its public API and ALES86's HalveNegError whole, inventing no new primitive and no new fault since min of x and zero has no illegal parameter; the family-whole law is proven by summing this rung against ALES139's stereo_halve on the same source (reconstructing the dry master per channel per sample), the image-partition law proves an out-of-phase master returns two disjoint non-positive channels whose sum is the negated magnitude while a one to two master keeps its ratio on the kept samples, every output non-positive on both, the map idempotent, the one refusal leaves both channels byte-for-byte untouched, and count equal to zero is the identity; siloed to lotus/, no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/stereo_halve_neg"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/stereo_halve_neg.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-stereo-halve-neg: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/stereo_halve_neg.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-stereo-halve-neg: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-stereo-halve-neg: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-stereo-halve-neg' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-stereo-halve-neg: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-stereo-halve-neg line"
